1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,447
Hi everyone, and thanks for joining.

2
00:00:02,886 --> 00:00:03,711
I am Haris.

3
00:00:04,543 --> 00:00:10,264
For most of this year, I have been running
a big group of AI agents in production.

4
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:14,470
And today, I want to share
what actually held up.

5
00:00:15,003 --> 00:00:19,883
Not the theory, the stuff
that survived real users.

6
00:00:20,916 --> 00:00:22,904
But let me start with a story.

7
00:00:23,828 --> 00:00:30,077
A bug that took us months to find,
and the strange part is nothing

8
00:00:30,078 --> 00:00:35,518
was broken so here is what I mean.

9
00:00:36,170 --> 00:00:39,868
As you can see on the screen,
every request succeeded.

10
00:00:40,710 --> 00:00:43,965
Every request our agent
sent to the model worked.

11
00:00:44,833 --> 00:00:47,970
No errors, no alerts,
nothing red anywhere.

12
00:00:48,735 --> 00:00:50,141
But look at that number.

13
00:00:50,891 --> 00:00:55,215
Our prompt cache hit
rate was 0% for months.

14
00:00:56,781 --> 00:01:02,565
Quick background in case you haven't used
it: prompt caching lets you reuse the

15
00:01:02,565 --> 00:01:08,136
parts of your prompts that doesn't change,
like your instructions, and you pay

16
00:01:08,152 --> 00:01:10,641
about a tenth of the price for that part.

17
00:01:11,588 --> 00:01:18,260
We had it turned on in the code, but the
code that talks to the model was joining

18
00:01:18,262 --> 00:01:25,542
the system prompt into one plain string,
and this, threw away the cache markers.

19
00:01:26,535 --> 00:01:31,417
So we paid full price on every
call, and nothing told us.

20
00:01:32,964 --> 00:01:37,053
We only found it when we looked
at the cost of single runs.

21
00:01:38,045 --> 00:01:39,454
The fix was a few lines.

22
00:01:40,054 --> 00:01:41,873
Finding it was the hard part

23
00:01:42,873 --> 00:01:50,076
And honestly, that bugs, bug, sums up
our what our running agents is like.

24
00:01:50,674 --> 00:01:55,387
They don't fail loudly, they
fail quietly, and they still give

25
00:01:55,392 --> 00:01:57,288
you something that looks fine.

26
00:01:58,564 --> 00:01:59,491
A bit about me.

27
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:07,337
I lead the AI side at Ruby AI, a US
startup that builds sales intelligence.

28
00:02:08,262 --> 00:02:13,707
I'm the main engineer on our agent
platform, and I run it in production.

29
00:02:15,104 --> 00:02:19,878
So the idea of this talk is the
line at the top of the slide.

30
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:25,720
Every pattern here removes a place
where the system can be wrong

31
00:02:26,239 --> 00:02:30,396
without telling you And you can
see the four patterns below it.

32
00:02:31,191 --> 00:02:34,904
One, the AI suggests, code decides.

33
00:02:35,384 --> 00:02:38,611
Second, quality you can see.

34
00:02:39,145 --> 00:02:41,205
Third, cost per run.

35
00:02:41,614 --> 00:02:43,984
And fourth, speed.

36
00:02:45,127 --> 00:02:48,696
At the end, I will give you
a short checklist you can

37
00:02:48,696 --> 00:02:50,556
take back to your own system

38
00:02:51,556 --> 00:02:58,755
So before the patterns, a quick picture
of what we run so the numbers make sense.

39
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:02,087
On the screen, you can see three numbers.

40
00:03:02,446 --> 00:03:04,531
More than 50 types of agents.

41
00:03:05,111 --> 00:03:10,989
They research companies, analyze
deals, write documents, prepare meeting

42
00:03:10,995 --> 00:03:13,504
briefs, and there is a chat assistant.

43
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:23,083
About 3,800 production runs a month, and
every single run is traced in Langfuse.

44
00:03:25,006 --> 00:03:30,048
They run on Claude Sonnet
and Haiku on Amazon Bedrock.

45
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:34,964
Most of our, most of our
agents have the same shape.

46
00:03:35,525 --> 00:03:40,817
A small config that says which
model, which tool, what it has to

47
00:03:40,833 --> 00:03:43,401
return, and what it depends on.

48
00:03:44,553 --> 00:03:49,619
Then a loop where it can call
all the tools with a hard limit

49
00:03:49,778 --> 00:03:55,835
on rounds, and it ends with a
structured output, not free text.

50
00:03:56,872 --> 00:04:00,970
Keep that shape in mind because the
first pattern builds right on it

51
00:04:01,970 --> 00:04:08,058
So pattern one, if I could only keep one
thing from this talk, it would be this

52
00:04:08,059 --> 00:04:11,036
one written right there on the screen.

53
00:04:11,405 --> 00:04:12,823
Let the AI suggest.

54
00:04:13,201 --> 00:04:15,194
Let the code decide.

55
00:04:16,071 --> 00:04:18,689
What d- what does that mean day to day?

56
00:04:19,204 --> 00:04:21,173
The three lines below say it.

57
00:04:21,916 --> 00:04:26,756
Math goes to code, formatting
goes to code, code, and changing

58
00:04:26,801 --> 00:04:29,224
a customer data goes to code.

59
00:04:30,644 --> 00:04:36,773
The model is great at reading, judging,
and writing, but it's not great

60
00:04:36,786 --> 00:04:39,392
at being exact every single time.

61
00:04:40,156 --> 00:04:44,611
And these three things have
to be exact every single time.

62
00:04:45,734 --> 00:04:48,228
Let me show you what each one looks like

63
00:04:49,228 --> 00:04:50,270
First, scores.

64
00:04:50,762 --> 00:04:52,041
We score deals.

65
00:04:52,591 --> 00:04:54,084
How healthy is the deal?

66
00:04:54,534 --> 00:04:57,401
How complete is our picture of it?

67
00:04:58,589 --> 00:05:04,562
It's really tempting to just ask the
model, "Give me a number out of 100."

68
00:05:05,147 --> 00:05:05,914
Please don't.

69
00:05:06,753 --> 00:05:12,317
As it says at the bottom here, it
will look fine, and it won't add up.

70
00:05:13,469 --> 00:05:17,945
So we split the work, and you
can follow it left to right.

71
00:05:18,930 --> 00:05:22,894
The model reads the evidence,
and for each factor, it gives

72
00:05:22,895 --> 00:05:24,909
a finding with a citation.

73
00:05:25,935 --> 00:05:26,649
Then code.

74
00:05:27,366 --> 00:05:32,527
A simple weighted formula does
the math, and the result is

75
00:05:32,529 --> 00:05:35,455
a score that always adds up.

76
00:05:36,567 --> 00:05:39,657
When someone asks, "Why is this 62?"

77
00:05:40,149 --> 00:05:45,466
We can show them exactly why
formatting works the same way.

78
00:05:46,408 --> 00:05:51,338
Our document agents used to write
the whole document in markdown.

79
00:05:52,253 --> 00:05:56,767
When, when I looked at their output,
that first number is what I found.

80
00:05:57,567 --> 00:06:02,794
Thirty-six to forty-five percent of
the output tokens were just formatting.

81
00:06:03,986 --> 00:06:08,318
Headings, bullets, tables,
and output tokens are the

82
00:06:08,330 --> 00:06:11,074
expensive ones and the slow ones.

83
00:06:11,950 --> 00:06:18,292
So now, as the boxes at the bottom
show, the model just returns the

84
00:06:18,330 --> 00:06:23,365
content as JSON, and the code--
and code builds the document.

85
00:06:25,037 --> 00:06:30,619
We moved twenty-two of our twenty-three
document agents this way, and

86
00:06:30,619 --> 00:06:32,688
the second number is the result.

87
00:06:33,290 --> 00:06:40,095
Output tokens went down between twenty-one
and seventy-eight percent per agent.

88
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:44,712
The layout is now the same every time, too

89
00:06:45,712 --> 00:06:49,457
The third rule is the one
that matters most for trust:

90
00:06:50,034 --> 00:06:51,743
changing a customer's data.

91
00:06:53,142 --> 00:06:58,229
Our chat assistant can update
things for a user, like moving a

92
00:06:58,243 --> 00:07:02,556
deal to the next stage, but follow
the three steps on the screen.

93
00:07:03,422 --> 00:07:07,625
One, the model proposes
the change as a card.

94
00:07:08,792 --> 00:07:13,133
Second, the user sees it and confirms.

95
00:07:13,974 --> 00:07:17,438
Third, code makes the
change, not the model.

96
00:07:19,366 --> 00:07:21,688
So where does that leave the model?

97
00:07:22,654 --> 00:07:30,380
It's in the line at the bottom, judging,
writing, and pulling facts out of a messy

98
00:07:30,414 --> 00:07:33,588
text with the code to back each one up.

99
00:07:34,563 --> 00:07:37,532
That's what's-- what it is good at.

100
00:07:38,713 --> 00:07:44,242
one ho-- one honest quid though,
moving things into code, into code,

101
00:07:44,607 --> 00:07:46,500
doesn't make them right by itself.

102
00:07:47,534 --> 00:07:52,717
The formula is only as good as
the evidence the model gives it.

103
00:07:53,469 --> 00:07:57,553
So you still need to check that
evidence, and that's exactly

104
00:07:57,556 --> 00:07:59,317
where the pattern two comes in

105
00:08:00,317 --> 00:08:04,877
Pattern two is about making
quality something you can see.

106
00:08:05,952 --> 00:08:11,714
Remember what I said at the start,
agents fail quietly, and the line

107
00:08:11,714 --> 00:08:14,324
on the screen says it simply.

108
00:08:15,042 --> 00:08:19,526
A run can finish, return perfect
JSON, and still be wrong.

109
00:08:20,356 --> 00:08:23,043
So you need a way to see that.

110
00:08:23,734 --> 00:08:29,606
We do it in two layers
Here they are side by side.

111
00:08:30,389 --> 00:08:32,187
On the left, code checks.

112
00:08:33,003 --> 00:08:36,911
On every single run,
they are cheap and fast.

113
00:08:38,144 --> 00:08:39,962
Is the output the right shape?

114
00:08:40,948 --> 00:08:45,604
Did any raw tool text leak
into what the user sees?

115
00:08:46,973 --> 00:08:50,475
Or any internal names, any errors?

116
00:08:51,756 --> 00:08:58,216
We also score how much we should
trust each run based on what

117
00:08:58,325 --> 00:09:00,483
it actually managed to find.

118
00:09:02,081 --> 00:09:05,491
On the right, AI judges on a sample.

119
00:09:06,444 --> 00:09:08,472
One judge checks grounding.

120
00:09:09,854 --> 00:09:15,436
That is, is every claim backed up
by what the agent actually found?

121
00:09:15,893 --> 00:09:21,846
We run it on 20% of runs for
about 30 customer-facing agents.

122
00:09:22,945 --> 00:09:26,623
Our second judge checks
one very specific thing.

123
00:09:27,321 --> 00:09:30,022
Is this even about the right company?

124
00:09:30,807 --> 00:09:33,240
That one runs on 10%.

125
00:09:34,227 --> 00:09:36,955
At the bottom of the card,
you can see the volume.

126
00:09:37,667 --> 00:09:44,575
Last month, about 1,700 judge, judge
scores a- across our environments,

127
00:09:45,258 --> 00:09:51,797
about 1,700 of them on production
runs, and each score about 700 of

128
00:09:51,798 --> 00:09:58,098
them on production runs, and each
score comes back in about 30 seconds.

129
00:09:58,963 --> 00:10:04,362
Now, setting this up taught
us a few things, the hard way.

130
00:10:04,945 --> 00:10:05,946
Here's the first one

131
00:10:06,946 --> 00:10:13,002
When we first turned on the grounding
judge, it gave one of our good analysis

132
00:10:13,570 --> 00:10:16,964
the score you see on the left, 0.2

133
00:10:17,369 --> 00:10:18,154
out of one.

134
00:10:19,156 --> 00:10:22,477
It looked like the agent
was making things up.

135
00:10:23,225 --> 00:10:24,041
It wasn't.

136
00:10:24,624 --> 00:10:26,049
The problem was the judge.

137
00:10:26,928 --> 00:10:31,764
As the label says, it only saw,
saw the prompt and the answer.

138
00:10:32,689 --> 00:10:37,413
It never saw what actually
agent had found with its tools.

139
00:10:38,175 --> 00:10:46,299
So every real fact the agent had
looked up, looked made up, made up

140
00:10:46,299 --> 00:10:54,174
to, to the judges So once we added
the tool outputs to the trace and the

141
00:10:54,175 --> 00:10:58,884
judge could see the real evidence,
the same answer scored what you see on

142
00:10:58,886 --> 00:11:02,045
the right side, zero point nine five.

143
00:11:02,849 --> 00:11:06,261
Eighteen of 19 claims backed up.

144
00:11:07,191 --> 00:11:09,236
So the lesson is the title of the slide.

145
00:11:09,741 --> 00:11:12,983
Judge what the agent found, not the prompt

146
00:11:13,983 --> 00:11:20,119
The second thing we learned is that
scores can lie in really boring ways.

147
00:11:20,856 --> 00:11:22,258
Three of them are on the screen.

148
00:11:23,147 --> 00:11:28,616
The first card, one of our checks
looked for a long random-looking

149
00:11:28,618 --> 00:11:31,182
string to catch leaked IDs.

150
00:11:32,218 --> 00:11:33,875
It fired on every single one.

151
00:11:34,592 --> 00:11:35,364
Every one.

152
00:11:36,765 --> 00:11:42,529
Turns out signed download links
are long random-looking strings.

153
00:11:43,457 --> 00:11:46,506
So a check that always fires is broken.

154
00:11:47,919 --> 00:11:49,810
The middle card is the opposite.

155
00:11:50,817 --> 00:11:53,512
The score that never moves is dead.

156
00:11:54,611 --> 00:11:56,210
Either way, it's telling you nothing.

157
00:11:57,077 --> 00:12:00,029
And the last card is
the fix for the judges.

158
00:12:00,756 --> 00:12:03,580
Plant a known bad case.

159
00:12:04,267 --> 00:12:09,487
Before we trusted a judge, we
gave it cases we knew were bad

160
00:12:10,006 --> 00:12:12,263
and checked that it caught them.

161
00:12:13,591 --> 00:12:17,055
Eight out of eight before it went live.

162
00:12:18,222 --> 00:12:23,791
But even so, a later test showed
ours was still too lenient.

163
00:12:24,673 --> 00:12:29,019
So calibration isn't a one-time
thing, and a lenient judge is

164
00:12:29,031 --> 00:12:34,530
worse than no judge because it
makes you feel safe when you aren't

165
00:12:35,530 --> 00:12:35,568
Yeah.

166
00:12:36,498 --> 00:12:38,774
And the third lesson is the humbling one.

167
00:12:39,520 --> 00:12:40,588
It's the whole slide.

168
00:12:41,547 --> 00:12:47,720
When we were comparing models, one of
them won on every score, every check

169
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:55,168
passed, every score green, and it had
written a whole brief about a wrong

170
00:12:55,177 --> 00:12:59,447
company No average will ever catch that.

171
00:13:00,395 --> 00:13:03,999
Someone had to actually read it.

172
00:13:04,616 --> 00:13:12,884
So today, a judge checks for exactly
that, and we still read outputs ourselves.

173
00:13:13,962 --> 00:13:20,009
That's also how we caught things
like a filing agent that invented

174
00:13:20,009 --> 00:13:25,783
financials or a news agent that
invented a multi-billion deal.

175
00:13:27,337 --> 00:13:31,134
And that-- And on top of
all of this, we have alerts.

176
00:13:32,024 --> 00:13:38,866
Errors above five an hour, grounding
below zero point four, wrong company

177
00:13:39,251 --> 00:13:45,389
z-- below zero point eight, and we
filed every alert once on purpose

178
00:13:46,063 --> 00:13:49,499
just to make sure it really reaches us

179
00:13:50,499 --> 00:13:52,151
So that's quality.

180
00:13:52,773 --> 00:13:56,732
It's one place agents fail quietly.

181
00:13:57,452 --> 00:14:04,160
Cost is another one, and that's the
pattern three: know what every run costs.

182
00:14:05,543 --> 00:14:06,945
It starts with measuring.

183
00:14:07,951 --> 00:14:14,796
As the slide says, every model call in
our system goes through one single place.

184
00:14:15,753 --> 00:14:22,436
That place works out what the call
cost, including cache reads and

185
00:14:22,436 --> 00:14:28,449
writes, and attaches it to the run,
and our numbers match the bill.

186
00:14:29,654 --> 00:14:31,867
And just doing that found things.

187
00:14:32,585 --> 00:14:34,080
Look at the line at the bottom.

188
00:14:34,540 --> 00:14:38,053
One agent was making
34 model calls per run.

189
00:14:39,028 --> 00:14:42,465
You can't fix what you
can't see run by run

190
00:14:43,465 --> 00:14:48,652
Over a few months, we've brought
the cost of an agent run down by

191
00:14:48,665 --> 00:14:52,581
the numbers you see, about 75%.

192
00:14:53,264 --> 00:15:00,153
One not-- one note on that, written just
under it, this is from staging data.

193
00:15:01,167 --> 00:15:05,524
We ran the same deal five
times as the changes shipped.

194
00:15:07,021 --> 00:15:10,337
The three cards are the
three of the biggest changes.

195
00:15:11,010 --> 00:15:13,762
On the left, fewer round trips.

196
00:15:14,425 --> 00:15:20,487
Our document agents worked in long
loops, eight to 12 rounds with

197
00:15:20,500 --> 00:15:24,383
the model, and every round sends
the whole conversation again.

198
00:15:24,927 --> 00:15:30,364
So you pay for it again and again,
where the agent already had what

199
00:15:30,365 --> 00:15:35,009
it needed, and we, and we moved
it to one main structured call.

200
00:15:35,788 --> 00:15:39,704
So eight to 12 rounds became two or three.

201
00:15:40,969 --> 00:15:45,368
In the middle, the silent cache
fix I told you about at the start.

202
00:15:46,051 --> 00:15:50,514
And on the right, cleaning
up tools results in the code

203
00:15:51,629 --> 00:15:53,103
before the model sees them.

204
00:15:53,877 --> 00:15:57,850
One tool gave back two to seven
thousand tokens of raw data.

205
00:15:59,249 --> 00:16:05,826
A small bit of code now turns that
into three to five hundred tokens with

206
00:16:05,837 --> 00:16:12,150
just what matters, and the answers
actually got more accurate, not less.

207
00:16:13,150 --> 00:16:15,417
Now here is something people don't expect.

208
00:16:16,326 --> 00:16:18,355
Caching can lose you money.

209
00:16:19,448 --> 00:16:20,737
Look at these three bars.

210
00:16:21,518 --> 00:16:24,165
The top one is the normal input price.

211
00:16:24,951 --> 00:16:30,977
The orange one is writing to
a five-minute cache, one and a

212
00:16:30,977 --> 00:16:33,060
quarter times the normal price.

213
00:16:33,545 --> 00:16:38,971
And that tiny bar at the bottom is reading
from the cache, a tenth of the price.

214
00:16:40,038 --> 00:16:46,171
So caching only pays off if you
read back enough of what you write.

215
00:16:47,316 --> 00:16:52,534
As it says below, the break-even
is about zero point two

216
00:16:52,556 --> 00:16:54,499
eight reads for every write.

217
00:16:56,391 --> 00:17:01,937
We had four agent that almost
never got a second read in time.

218
00:17:02,936 --> 00:17:10,889
For them, caching was costing more than it
saved, so we turned it off for these four.

219
00:17:12,243 --> 00:17:18,711
Before you assume caching is helping,
check your real reads and writes

220
00:17:19,712 --> 00:17:24,377
And one more things about the cost:
the platform can change under you.

221
00:17:25,672 --> 00:17:30,261
When you add a new cache point,
Bedrock only looks back a

222
00:17:30,262 --> 00:17:32,064
short way to find the old one.

223
00:17:33,072 --> 00:17:37,667
The card on the left is what
I measured in early October.

224
00:17:38,438 --> 00:17:44,087
Every tool call and every
tool result counted as a step.

225
00:17:45,025 --> 00:17:49,417
So a turn with lots of parallel
tool calls lost the cache.

226
00:17:50,715 --> 00:17:55,572
The card on right is what I found when
I tested again at the end of October.

227
00:17:56,362 --> 00:17:57,891
That had changed.

228
00:17:58,711 --> 00:18:06,613
A group of parallel tool calls now
counts as one step, which matches how

229
00:18:06,676 --> 00:18:10,083
Anthropic's own documents describe it.

230
00:18:11,042 --> 00:18:15,910
That's good news for everyone, but the
lesson is the line below the cards.

231
00:18:16,760 --> 00:18:21,279
Keep a live check for the
things your cost depend on.

232
00:18:22,413 --> 00:18:28,233
I maintain a small open source
tool for exactly this called Cache

233
00:18:28,246 --> 00:18:32,208
Canary, so I retest it regularly.

234
00:18:32,985 --> 00:18:36,325
Whatever you use, test it yourself.

235
00:18:37,031 --> 00:18:42,052
Don't trust last quarter's
blog post, including mine

236
00:18:43,052 --> 00:18:49,324
Before we get to speed, I want to be
honest about what didn't work because

237
00:18:49,339 --> 00:18:52,285
these four taught me just as much.

238
00:18:53,144 --> 00:18:58,425
Top left, we tried a lower
effort settings to save money.

239
00:18:59,378 --> 00:19:07,182
It actually cost more, twenty-four to
sixty-four percent more input because

240
00:19:07,193 --> 00:19:13,086
the model started making its tool
calls one by one instead of together.

241
00:19:14,404 --> 00:19:17,168
Top right, we built a cache warmer.

242
00:19:17,715 --> 00:19:22,630
After some other changes, it could
never get a cache hit, but its

243
00:19:22,686 --> 00:19:25,386
logs still said warmed every time.

244
00:19:26,170 --> 00:19:31,296
We only found out because we
measured the hits, not the log lines.

245
00:19:33,006 --> 00:19:36,346
Bottom left, we doubled
our parallel workers.

246
00:19:36,975 --> 00:19:40,749
On one sample, it looked
sixty-four percent better.

247
00:19:41,230 --> 00:19:46,705
On three samples, it was sixteen
percent worse, so we reverted it.

248
00:19:47,183 --> 00:19:48,765
One sample lies.

249
00:19:50,008 --> 00:19:55,186
And the bottom right, we tested a
newer model whose tokenizer turned

250
00:19:55,439 --> 00:20:02,310
our prompts into about forty-eight
percent more tokens with no gain

251
00:20:02,310 --> 00:20:05,279
in quality, so we didn't switch

252
00:20:06,279 --> 00:20:09,103
Which brings me to the
last pattern, speed.

253
00:20:10,350 --> 00:20:14,881
Researching a new company took our
system about thirty-two minutes.

254
00:20:15,450 --> 00:20:21,458
Users were waiting, and the idea on the
table was what you see on the screen.

255
00:20:22,426 --> 00:20:26,554
Send users text updates to
keep them busy while they wait.

256
00:20:27,546 --> 00:20:34,255
But before building that, I asked the
question in orange: Why thirty-two

257
00:20:34,266 --> 00:20:35,993
minutes in the first place?

258
00:20:37,004 --> 00:20:41,255
So I looked at the timeline of real runs.

259
00:20:41,679 --> 00:20:42,829
It wasn't the model.

260
00:20:43,745 --> 00:20:45,415
We weren't being throttled.

261
00:20:46,078 --> 00:20:51,957
Thirteen agents were simply running
one after another, one step at a time

262
00:20:52,957 --> 00:20:59,662
You can see it at the top of the
slide, 13 agents in a line, one

263
00:20:59,702 --> 00:21:04,970
after another, and most of those
agents didn't need each other.

264
00:21:06,459 --> 00:21:12,426
So I checked what each agent really
needs and grouped them into levels.

265
00:21:13,151 --> 00:21:14,436
That's the picture below.

266
00:21:15,428 --> 00:21:21,047
The first level had seven agents that
didn't depend on any other agent.

267
00:21:22,110 --> 00:21:28,240
The second had four agents that
needed something from the first,

268
00:21:29,149 --> 00:21:31,624
and the third had only two.

269
00:21:32,653 --> 00:21:35,557
Everything in a level runs together.

270
00:21:36,083 --> 00:21:41,537
And when one lev-- when one level
is done, the next one starts.

271
00:21:42,148 --> 00:21:45,631
Same agents, same model,
only the order changed.

272
00:21:46,947 --> 00:21:49,481
And the result is the
big number on the right.

273
00:21:50,067 --> 00:21:56,200
The median went from 32 and
a half minutes to about 10.7,

274
00:21:57,121 --> 00:22:04,698
and it stayed around 11 over
700 production runs in 45 days.

275
00:22:06,292 --> 00:22:13,429
One small side note: caching helped our
cost a lot, but it wasn't really a speed

276
00:22:13,429 --> 00:22:17,272
fix, so don't count on it for that.

277
00:22:18,593 --> 00:22:20,796
And we never built the
text message feature.

278
00:22:21,304 --> 00:22:27,226
So draw the dependency graph before
you build something to hide the wait

279
00:22:28,226 --> 00:22:31,392
So let me pull, pull,
pull it all together.

280
00:22:31,929 --> 00:22:37,929
These are the seven things
I would again on day one, I

281
00:22:37,930 --> 00:22:39,938
would do again on the day one.

282
00:22:40,673 --> 00:22:46,093
first, put math, formatting,
and data changes in the code.

283
00:22:46,685 --> 00:22:52,193
Second, code checks on every
run and judges on a sample.

284
00:22:53,413 --> 00:22:58,680
Third, judge against what
the agent actually found.

285
00:22:59,812 --> 00:23:05,275
Fourth, fire every alert once on purpose.

286
00:23:06,870 --> 00:23:12,542
Fifth, price you every run
yourself, cache included.

287
00:23:14,029 --> 00:23:18,707
Six, turn caching off
where it loses money.

288
00:23:19,782 --> 00:23:27,005
And seven, draw the graph before you
build anything to hide a slow system.

289
00:23:28,231 --> 00:23:34,258
On the right, there are a few more, so
feel free to take a screenshot of this one

290
00:23:35,258 --> 00:23:41,225
If you take away one thing today,
let it be the line on the screen.

291
00:23:42,243 --> 00:23:49,999
Agents usually don't fail loudly, and
every pattern I showed you is about

292
00:23:50,041 --> 00:23:56,803
removing one more place where the system
can be wrong without even telling you.

293
00:23:58,086 --> 00:24:04,114
So thank you so much for listening,
and thanks to Conf42 for having me.

294
00:24:04,855 --> 00:24:08,972
If you are running agents in
production and want to swap

295
00:24:08,972 --> 00:24:11,515
notes, my LinkedIn is right there.

296
00:24:12,364 --> 00:24:17,621
And if you want to check your own
prompt caching on Bedrock, Cache

297
00:24:17,627 --> 00:24:20,615
Canary is free at cachecanary.com.

298
00:24:21,478 --> 00:24:24,818
I would really love to
hear what's working for you
